{"selected_paradigms": ["clique_density", "spectral", "homophily_violation", "community"], "rationale": "Landmarker tails, not raw means, are the deciding evidence: clique_density shows the sharpest elbow of all paradigms (p50 0.024 -> p99 0.44, ~18x jump, mean 0.057/std 0.09), consistent with the dense, moderately clustered structure (density 0.0256, avg_degree 66.51, clustering_coeff 0.1229) that would let abnormally dense/triangle-rich pockets stand out against baseline closure. spectral has the largest z-separation (mean 0.048, std 0.070, p99 0.376, ~4.7 std out) and the graph's spectral_gap of 7.40 with 10 well-separated communities (modularity 0.3612) gives that frequency signal a coherent low-frequency baseline to deviate from. homophily_violation is well supported because global homophily is only moderate (0.5453) on very high-dimensional, extremely sparse features (dim 8189, sparsity 0.99) -- most nodes partially agree with neighbors, but the landmarker's sharp tail (p50 0.069 -> p99 0.43, ~6x) shows a distinct minority strongly mismatching. community complements these with a global view: modularity 0.3612 across 10 communities is strong enough that a modularity-residual signal is meaningful, and its landmarker tail (p50 0.066 -> p99 0.41, ~6x, ~4.5 std) confirms a distinguishable population failing to fit any community. local_affinity was excluded despite being a common default: its landmarker is saturated (mean 0.86, std only 0.078, p99 only ~1.4 std above mean), likely because avg_degree 66.5 averages neighbor similarity toward a uniformly high baseline, destroying discriminative power; density and attr_struct_mismatch were excluded as weaker/noisier variants that would largely duplicate the community and homophily_violation signals already selected."}
```

